ed police officer is outside of  his or her jurisdiction in some other  jurisdiction and witnesses a crime it  makes sense for that individual to  effect a citizen's arrest or a  shopkeeper suspecting someone of  shoplifting  it makes sense if the person has been  trained to affect a citizen's arrest but  not use force  but beyond that it seems to me these  laws ought to be abolished this is  a a doctrine whose time should have  passed  not just years ago but probably  centuries ago  and yet we see it uh used by all sorts  of groups across the country from  so-called neighborhood patrols to  efforts to arrest public officials  we do see it and and that's where we  also see abuses uh it makes no sense for  an ordinary individual to go up to  someone whom he or she suspects of  having committed a crime  and say  i'm going to kill you i'm going to shoot  you i'm going to arrest you  bad things can happen  both to the individual who is arrested  and to the arrest or  when i say bad things can happen i mean  serious injury or death  and if we're not talking at that level  of seriousness the person can be charged  with a crime or can be sued in civil  court for monetary damages uh for for  false arrests  and so for instance the uh plot to  arrest the governor of michigan other  efforts there was an attack on a law  professor all because of disagreements  over what was being taught uh what are  the most uh sort of telling examples in  your mind of of the abuse of this  citizen's arrest doctrine  well the the most telling examples are  the cases in georgia  but you mentioned the  citizens arrest of a professor in  virginia that happened in 2014 and  that's what got me interested in this  issue i thought if a person can come off  the street  go into a classroom or a class is being  taught go up to the professor  and say uh you're under arrest i'm i'm  executing a citizen's arrest and if you  don't comply i'm going to spread you  with pepper spray and that's exactly  what happened that makes no sense people  should not be taking the law into their  own hands so you see in the hannah payne  case in georgia  where she uh effected a citizen's arrest  on someone who had committed a hit and  run  while driving  she thought that she was allowed to do  it she went up to him and she said i'm  going to shoot you and she shot him in  the chest and killed him was that a  lawful citizen's arrest no  because  she thought that she was getting someone  or arresting someone who had committed a  felony  which is allowed under the old georgia  statute that was in effect at the time  it has since been changed but in fact  and again i guarantee she would not have  known this uh a hit and run in which  there were no injuries in georgia is a  misdemeanor it's not a felony so it was  not a good citizen's arrest  but there are notions of the citizens  arrest doctrine around the country and  people try to invoke it as their defense  when they get caught doing something  they do and it should not be a good  defense  uh and and in fact it turns into a house  of cards because the starting point is  they say i i had the right to commit a  citizen's arrest and then during the  arrest the person i was arresting  attacked me so i used self-defense and i  killed that individual  it's a house of cards because if the  citizens arrest was invalid then the  arrest door is an aggressor and the  aggressor does not have the right to  self-defense  so you would recommend these laws  essentially be abolished across the  country  absolutely yes uh that their time has  passed  all right professor iowa robins from  american university thank you so much  for being with us here tonight thank you  very much for having me  coming up next this week's winners and  losers in the media see if you agree  with the editors of mediaite and what  they had to say about cnn bombshell  report on the tensions between president  biden and vice president harris you're  watching brian ross investigat